CHAPTER 70: GENERAL PROVISIONS
Section
   
70.01   Definitions
   
70.02   Authority to adopt traffic-control regulations
   
70.03   Application of traffic-control regulations
   
70.04   Deposit of fines into general fund
   
70.05   Vehicle inspection fees
§ 70.01 DEFINITIONS.
   For the purpose of this title, concerning traffic-control regulations, the
following definitions shall apply unless the context clearly indicates or
requires a different meaning.
   ALLEY. A street intended to provide access to the rear or side of lots or
buildings in urban districts and not intended for the purpose of through
vehicular traffic.
   BICYCLE. Any foot-propelled vehicle, irrespective of the number of wheels in
contact with the ground.
   BUREAU. The Bureau of Motor Vehicles of this state acting directly or
through its duly authorized officers and agents.
   BUS.
      (1)   A motor vehicle designed for carrying passengers for hire and used
for the transportation of persons; or
      (2)   A motor vehicle, other than a taxicab, designed or used for the
transportation of persons for compensation.
   BUSINESS DISTRICT. The territory contiguous to and including a street when
50% or more of the frontage thereon for a distance of 500 feet or more is
occupied by buildings in use for business.
   CROSSWALK.
      (1)   The part of a roadway at an intersection included within the
connections of the lateral lines of the sidewalks on opposite sides of the
street measured from the curbs or, in the absence of curbs, from the edges of
the traversable roadway; or
      (2)   Any portion of a roadway at an intersection or elsewhere distinctly
indicated for pedestrian crossing by lines or other markings on the surface.
   DRIVER. A person who drives or is in actual physical control of a vehicle.
   GROSS WEIGHT. The weight of a vehicle without load plus the weight of any
load thereon.
   HIGHWAY. A street.
   INTERSECTION.
      (1)   (a)   The area embraced within the prolongation or connection of
the lateral curb lines or, if none, the lateral boundary lines of the roadways
of two streets that join one another at or approximately at right angles; or
         (b)   The area within which vehicles traveling upon different streets
joining at any other angle may come into conflict.
      (2)   Where a street includes two roadways 30 feet or more apart, then
every crossing of each roadway of that divided street by an intersecting street
shall be regarded as a separate INTERSECTION. In the event the intersecting
street also includes two roadways 30 feet or more apart, then every crossing of
two roadways of the streets shall be regarded as a separate INTERSECTION.
   LANED STREET. A street the roadway of which is divided in three or more
clearly marked lanes for vehicular traffic.
   MOTOR VEHICLE. Every vehicle, except a motorized bicycle, that is self-
propelled, and every vehicle that is propelled by electric power obtained from
overhead trolley wires, but not operated upon rails.
   MOTORCYCLE. A motor vehicle, except a farm tractor or a motorized bicycle,
with motive power, having a seat or saddle for the use of the rider and
designed to travel on not more than three wheels in contact with the ground.
   MOTORIZED BICYCLE. A bicycle with operable pedals that may be propelled by
human power, by an internal combustion engine or battery-powered motor, or by
both, and when powered by an internal combustion engine, having an engine
rating of no more than 1.5 brake horsepower and a cylinder capacity not
exceeding 50 cubic centimeters, an automatic transmission, and a maximum design
speed of not more than 25 miles per hour on a flat surface.
   M.P.H. Miles per hour.
   OWNER.
      (1)   The person who holds the legal title of a vehicle;
      (2)   The conditional vendee or lessee in the event a vehicle is the
subject of an agreement for the conditional sale or lease of the vehicle with
the right of purchase upon performance of the conditions stated in the
agreement and with an immediate right of possession in the conditional vendee
or lessee; or
      (3)   The mortgagor, in the event the mortgagor of a vehicle is entitled
to possession.
   PARK or PARKING. The standing of a vehicle, whether occupied or not,
otherwise than temporarily for the purpose of and while actually engaged in
loading or unloading property or passengers.
   PERSON. A natural person, firm, copartnership, association, or corporation.
   POLICE OFFICER. An officer authorized to direct or regulate traffic or to
make arrests for violations of traffic regulations.
   PRIVATE BUS. A motor vehicle that is designed and constructed for the
accommodation of passengers and that is used for transportation of passengers
by a religious, fraternal, charitable, or benevolent organization, by a youth
association, or by a public or private institution of higher education. The
term PRIVATE BUS includes the chassis, the body, or both the chassis and body
of any such vehicle. The term PRIVATE BUS does not include a vehicle having a
seating capacity of 15 persons or less, a school bus, or a bus used to carry
passengers for hire.
   PRIVATE DRIVEWAY. A private road.
   PRIVATE ROAD. A way or place in private ownership that is used for vehicular
travel by the owner and those who have express or implied permission from the
owner, but not by other persons.
   RAILROAD SIGN or RAILROAD SIGNAL. A sign, signal, or device erected by
authority of a public body or official or by a railroad and intended to give
notice of the presence of railroad tracks or the approach of a railroad train.
   RESIDENCE DISTRICT. The territory contiguous to and including a street not
comprising a business district when the property on that street for a distance
of 500 feet or more is in the main improved with residences or residences and
buildings used for business.
   RIGHT-OF-WAY. The privilege of the immediate use of a street.
   ROADWAY. The portion of a street improved, designed, or ordinarily used for
vehicular travel.
   ROUNDABOUT. An intersection characterized by a circulatory roadway and yield
control of entering traffic.
   SCHOOL BUS. A motor vehicle:
      (1)   Owned by a public or governmental agency and operated for the
transportation of children to or from school; or
      (2)   Privately owned and operated for compensation for the
transportation of children to and from school.
   SIDEWALK. The portion of a street between the curb lines or the lateral
lines of a roadway and the adjacent property lines intended for the use of
pedestrians.
   STAND or STANDING. The halting of a vehicle, whether occupied or not,
otherwise than temporarily for the purpose of and while actually engaged in
receiving or discharging passengers.
   STOP or STOPPING. When prohibited, means any halting, even momentarily, of a
vehicle, whether occupied or not, except when necessary to avoid conflict with
other traffic or in compliance with the directions of a police officer or
traffic-control device.
   STREET. The entire width between the boundary lines of every way publicly
maintained when any part thereof is open to the use of the public for purposes
of vehicular travel.
   THROUGH STREET. A street or portion thereof at the entrance to which
vehicular traffic from intersecting streets is required by law to yield the
right-of-way to vehicles on the THROUGH STREET in obedience to either a stop
sign or a yield sign erected as provided in the chapters of this title
concerning traffic-control regulations.
   TRAFFIC. Pedestrians, ridden or herded animals, street cars, vehicles, and
other conveyances, either singly or together, while using any street for
purposes of travel.
   TRAFFIC-CONTROL DEVICE. A sign, signal, marking, or device, including a
railroad advance warning sign, not inconsistent with I.C. 9-21-1-4, placed or
erected by authority of a public body or official having jurisdiction for the
purpose of regulating, warning, or guiding traffic.
   TRAFFIC-CONTROL SIGNAL. A device, not inconsistent with I.C. 9-21-1-4,
whether manually, electrically, or mechanically operated, by which traffic is
alternately directed to stop and to proceed.
   TRUCK. Every motor vehicle designed, used, or maintained primarily for the
transportation of property.
   URBAN DISTRICT. The territory contiguous to and including any street that is
built up with structures devoted to business, industry, or dwelling houses
situated at intervals of less than 200 feet for a distance of one-quarter of a
mile or more.
   VEHICLE. Every device in, upon, or by which any person or property is or may
be transported or drawn upon a highway, except devices used exclusively upon
stationary rails or tracks.
(1985 Code, § 440-5) (Ord. 126, passed 8-2-1948; Am. Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-
1980; Am. Ord. 2020-16, passed 4-27-2020)
§ 70.02 AUTHORITY TO ADOPT TRAFFIC-CONTROL REGULATIONS.
   (A)   The town has the authority under I.C. 9-21-1-2a to adopt by ordinance
traffic regulations with respect to streets and highways under its jurisdiction
so long as they do not conflict with or duplicate the provisions of a state
statute.
   (B)   The town, with respect to streets and highways under its jurisdiction
and within the reasonable exercise of its police power, has the authority under
I.C. 9-21-1-3 to:
      (1)   Regulate the standing or parking of vehicles;
      (2)   Regulate traffic by means of police officers or traffic-control
signals;
      (3)   Regulate or prohibit processions or assemblages on the highways;
      (4)   Designate particular highways as one- way highways and require that
all vehicles thereon be moved in one specific direction;
      (5)   Regulate the speed of vehicles in public parks;
      (6)   Designate any highway as a through highway and require that all
vehicles stop before entering or crossing the same;
      (7)   Designate any intersection as a stop intersection and require all
vehicles to stop at one or more entrances to that intersection;
      (8)   Restrict the use of highways as authorized in I.C. 9-21-4-7;
      (9)   Regulate the operation of bicycles and require their licensing,
including requiring a registration fee;
      (10)   Regulate or prohibit the turning of vehicles at intersections;
      (11)   Alter the prima facie or maximum speed limits as authorized in
I.C. 9-21-5;
      (12)   Adopt traffic regulations governing traffic control on public
school grounds when requested to do so by the governing body of a school
corporation; and
      (13)   Adopt other traffic regulations as are specifically authorized by
I.C. 9-21-1 or other provisions of the Indiana Code.
   (C)   An ordinance or traffic regulation adopted under divisions (B)(4)
through (B)(12) above shall be effective when signs giving notice of such a
traffic regulation are posted upon or at the entrances to the highway or part
of the highway affected, as may be most appropriate.
   (D)   The town has and may exercise any additional powers and authority to
adopt traffic-control regulations and other regulations with respect to streets
and highways within the town as have been granted by state statute or
regulation.
(1985 Code, § 440-10) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980)
§ 70.03 APPLICATION OF TRAFFIC-CONTROL REGULATIONS.
   (A)   Except when a different place is specifically referred to, the
provisions of the chapters of this title relating to the operation of vehicles
refer exclusively to the operation of vehicles upon streets, including streets
of a residential subdivision and alleys, regardless of who maintains them.
   (B)   The provisions of the chapters of this title applicable to the drivers
of vehicles on a street shall apply to the drivers of all vehicles owned or
operated by the U.S., this state, or any county, city, town, district, or other
political subdivision of the state, subject to those specific exceptions as are
set forth in I.C. 9-21-1-8 with respect to authorized emergency vehicles.
   (C)   Unless specifically made applicable, the provisions of the chapters of
this title concerning traffic-control regulations shall not apply to persons,
teams, motor vehicles, and other equipment while actually engaged in work on
the surface of a street, but shall apply to those persons and vehicles when
traveling to or from work.
   (D)   The provisions of the chapters of this title concerning traffic-
control regulations supplement rather than replace state traffic laws and
regulations and shall not relieve any driver, pedestrian, or other person from
the duty to observe the requirements of state traffic laws and regulations.
(1985 Code, § 440-15)
§ 70.04 DEPOSIT OF FINES INTO GENERAL FUND.
   (A)   All monies or fines collected for violations of traffic ordinances
shall be deposited in the Law Enforcement Continuing Education Fund.
   (B)   All monies or fines collected for violations of parking ordinances
shall be deposited and equally divided between the town’s General Fund and the
Law Enforcement Continuing Education Fund.
   (C)   All monies or funds collected for violations of any other regulation
or ordinances adopted by the town shall be deposited in the town’s General Fund
unless specified otherwise in the ordinance.
(1985 Code, §440-20) (Am. Ord. 99-9, passed 4-12-1999; Am. Ord. 06-16, passed
9-11-2006)
§ 70.05 VEHICLE INSPECTION FEES.
   (A)   Pursuant to I.C. 9-17-2-12 and 9-29-4, any town police officer who
performs a vehicle inspection in accordance with that code section shall charge
a fee of $5 per inspection.
   (B)   The police officer shall prepare those forms required by I.C. 9-17-2-
12.
   (C)   The town police officer shall remit to the Town Clerk-Treasurer an
official receipt for the remittance specifying on each receipt the number of
general receipts included.
   (D)   These fees shall be remitted to the Town Clerk-Treasurer at least once
a week.
   (E)   The Town Clerk-Treasurer shall deposit these fees in the Law
Enforcement Continuing Education Fund.
   (F)   The Town Council may from time to time appropriate the money collected
from the inspections to be used for law enforcement purposes.
(1985 Code, Ch. 444) (Ord. 88-11, passed 8-15-1988; Am. Ord. 06-16, passed 9-
11-2006)
CHAPTER 71: TRAFFIC RULES
Section
Traffic Rules
   
71.01   Speed limits
   
71.02   Stop intersections; regulations
   
71.03   Through streets; stop and yield regulations
   
71.04   Roundabouts
Impeding Traffic Flow; Parades and Solicitations
   
71.15   Permit to parade, solicit, or impede traffic flow required
   
71.16   Permit issuance procedure
   
71.17   Permit fee
   
71.18   Denial or restriction; appeals
   
71.19   Violations
TRAFFIC RULES
§ 71.01 SPEED LIMITS.
   (A)   It has been determined on the basis of an engineering and traffic
investigation that the statutory maximum speed limits of 30 miles per hour in
an urban district and 55 miles per hour on the interstate defense network of
dual highways and in other locations, as permitted by state law, are either
greater or less than is reasonable and safe under the conditions found to exist
on the streets or parts of streets described in the schedule of maximum speed
limits in
Ch. 73, Sched. I, of this code and that it is necessary to alter the statutory
maximum speed limits on those streets or parts of streets.
   (B)   The maximum speed limits established in the schedule of maximum speed
limits in
Ch. 73, Sched. I, are determined and declared to be reasonable and safe maximum
limits for the streets and parts of streets listed in the schedule.
   (C)   The maximum speed limits established in the schedule of maximum speed
limits in
Ch. 73, Sched. I, shall be effective at all times, unless otherwise indicated,
when appropriate signs giving notice of the maximum speed limits are erected on
the streets and parts of streets listed in the schedule.
   (D)   Except when state law requires a lower speed, the maximum speed limits
specified in the schedule of maximum speed limits in
Ch. 73, Sched. I, shall be maximum lawful speeds, and no person shall drive a
vehicle on the designated streets and parts of streets in excess of the maximum
speed limits.
   (E)   A person who violates this section commits a class C ordinance
violation, and a judgment may be entered against the violator.
(1985 Code, § 441-5) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 71.02 STOP INTERSECTIONS; REGULATIONS.
   (A)   It has been determined on the basis of an engineering and traffic
investigation that certain intersections should be designated as stop
intersections and that stop signs should be erected at the entrance to the stop
intersections on one or more intersecting streets.
   (B)   The intersections described in the schedule of stop intersections in
Ch. 73, Sched. II, of this code are designated as stop intersections. Stop
signs shall be erected at the entrance to a stop intersection on one or more
intersecting streets.
   (C)   Every driver shall stop in obedience to a stop sign erected at the
entrance to a stop intersection before entering the intersection, except when
directed to proceed by a police officer or traffic-control signal, and shall
comply with all other requirements of state law concerning the duties of
drivers of vehicles at the entrance to a stop intersection.
   (D)   A person who violates this section commits a class C ordinance
violation, and a judgment may be entered against the violator.
(1985 Code, § 442-20) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 71.03 THROUGH STREETS; STOP AND YIELD REGULATIONS.
   (A)   It has been determined on the basis of an engineering and traffic
investigation that certain streets or parts of streets should be designated as
through streets and that stop or yield signs should be erected at the entrances
to the through streets on specified intersecting streets.
   (B)   The streets described as through streets in the schedule of stop
intersections at through streets in
Ch. 73, Sched. III, of this code and the schedule of yield intersections at
through streets in
Ch. 73, Sched. IV, are designated as through streets. Stop signs shall be
erected at the entrances to through streets on the intersecting stop streets
specified in the schedule of stop intersections at through streets in
Ch. 73, Sched. III. Yield signs shall be erected at the entrances to through
streets on the intersecting yield streets specified in the schedule of yield
intersections at through streets in
Ch. 73, Sched. IV.
   (C)   Every driver shall stop in obedience to a stop sign erected at the
entrance to a through street on an intersecting stop street before entering the
intersection and shall yield in obedience to a yield sign erected at the
entrance to a through street on an intersecting yield street before entering
the intersection, except when directed to proceed by a police officer or
traffic- control signal, and shall comply with all other requirements of state
law concerning the duties of drivers of vehicles at the entrance to a through
highway.
   (D)   A person who violates this section commits a class C ordinance
violation, and a judgment may be entered against the violator.
(1985 Code, § 442-5) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980) Penalty, see §
10.99
§ 71.04 ROUNDABOUTS.
   (A)   It has been determined on the basis of an engineering and traffic
investigation that certain streets or parts of streets should be designated as
roundabouts and that yield signs should be erected at the entrances to the
roundabouts.
   (B)   Yield signs shall be erected at the entrances to the roundabouts on
the intersecting yield streets specified in the schedule of yield intersections
at through streets in Ch. 73, Sched. V.
   (C)   Every driver shall yield the right-of- way to all other vehicles
already in the roundabout, even if entering the right lane (or outside lane) of
the roundabout and shall turn on his or her turn signal to indicate an
impending exit.
(Ord. 2020-16, passed 4-27-2020) Penalty, see §
10.99
IMPEDING TRAFFIC FLOW; PARADES AND SOLICITATIONS
§ 71.15 PERMIT TO PARADE, SOLICIT, OR IMPEDE TRAFFIC FLOW REQUIRED.
   No person or group of persons shall parade, solicit, or engage in any
activity that tends to impede the flow of traffic upon the streets or alleys of
the town unless there shall first be obtained a permit to do so as provided in
this subchapter.
(1985 Code, § 480-5) (Ord. 77-214, passed 10-17-1977)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 71.16 PERMIT ISSUANCE PROCEDURE.
   Any person or group of persons desiring to parade or solicit as set out in §
71.15 of this code shall first apply to the Town Marshal for a permit to do so.
The Town Marshal shall deny or restrict these permits so as to best accommodate
the interests of that person or group of persons and the town’s necessity to
prevent interference with the free flow of traffic upon the streets.
(1985 Code, § 480-10) (Ord. 77-214, passed 10-17-1977)
§ 71.17 PERMIT FEE.
   (A)   The fee for a permit must be paid by the applicant before the permit
may be issued by the Town Marshal.
   (B)   The fee for the permit shall be as follows:
      (1)   For one day or any fraction thereof: $10; and
      (2)   For each additional day or fraction thereof: $10.
(1985 Code, § 480-15) (Ord. 77-214, passed 10-17-1977)
§ 71.18 DENIAL OR RESTRICTION; APPEALS.
   If an application is denied or, as restricted, is unacceptable to the
applicant, the applicant has the right to appeal the decision of the Town
Marshal to the Town Council at a regularly scheduled public meeting of the
Council.
(1985 Code, § 480-20) (Ord. 77-214, passed 10-17-1977)
§ 71.19 VIOLATIONS.
   Any person violating any of the provisions of this subchapter or the
restrictions of a permit issued under this subchapter commits a class C
ordinance violation and shall be fined. Each and every day this subchapter or
restrictions on a permit issued under this subchapter are violated shall be
deemed a separate offense.
(1985 Code, § 480-25) (Ord. 77-214, passed 10-17-1977)
Penalty, see § 10.99
CHAPTER 72: PARKING REGULATIONS
Section
   
72.01   Parking restrictions
   
72.02   No parking areas; parking prohibited
   
72.03   Temporary visitor parking permit
   
72.04   Emergency conditions; parking prohibition
   
72.05   Violations; enforcement
   
72.06   Unpaid fines; impoundment
§ 72.01 PARKING RESTRICTIONS.
   (A)   All vehicles shall be parked according to the guide lines painted upon
either the pavement or the curbing.
   (B)   No vehicle shall be parked:
      (1)   Within 15 feet in either direction of a fire hydrant located at any
place within the limits of the town;
      (2)   Within ten feet of the center line of the entrance of any public
building or church;
      (3)   Within 20 feet of any street or highway intersection;
      (4)   In alleys within the limits of the town;
      (5)   So as to obstruct any alley or driveway; or
      (6)   On that area located between the sidewalk and the town street or
state highway.
   (C)   Any person, firm, corporation, operator, or driver who violates this
section commits a class E ordinance violation, and a judgment shall be entered
for each violation.
(1985 Code, § 443-5) (Ord. 126, passed 8-2-1948; Am. Ord. 87-13, passed 6-1-
1987)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 72.02 NO PARKING AREAS; PARKING PROHIBITED.
   (A)   When signs are erected giving notice thereof, no person shall park a
vehicle at any time upon any of the streets or parts of streets designated as
no parking areas and described in the schedule of no parking areas in
Ch. 74, Sched. I, of this code unless that person is granted a temporary
visitor’s permit by the Town Marshal or his or her staff as provided in §
72.03 of this code.
   (B)   A person who violates the provisions of this section commits a class E
ordinance violation and shall be fined. In addition, the person’s vehicle is
subject to being towed at the discretion of the Police Department if it creates
a hazard.
   (C)   An administrative fee shall be assessed on towed vehicles to help
offset costs associated with the implementation, enforcement, and
administration of the Traffic Code.
      (1)   If a vehicle is removed and impounded pursuant to the Traffic Code,
or for violations of the Indiana Code or other authorized police impoundments,
then in addition to any towing and storage charges assessed by the towing
company used, an administrative fee of $25 shall be levied when the vehicle
owner or last operator obtains a copy of the abandoned/impounded vehicle report
from the Police Department.
      (2)   The administrative fee shall be deposited and equally divided
between the town’s General Fund and the Law Enforcement Continuing Education
Fund in accordance with §
70.04(B).
(1985 Code, § 443-10) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980; Am. Ord. 85-6, passed 4-15-
1985; Am. Ord. 2013-07, passed 8-12-2013) Penalty, see §
10.99
§ 72.03 TEMPORARY VISITOR PARKING PERMIT.
   (A)   A person desiring to be entitled to visitor’s parking shall contact
the Town Marshal or his or her agent, who shall have absolute discretion as to
whether or not that visitor parking should be permitted. Visitor’s parking will
not be permitted for longer than 48 hours. If additional time is required, the
individual must contact the Town Marshal for an extension. An extension shall
only be for a period of 48 hours. The Town Marshal may grant more than one
extension.
   (B)   The Town Marshal or his or her staff shall log every request for
parking and whether or not the request was granted. The log book shall also
specify the location where visitor’s parking was permitted. In determining
whether or not to grant a permit, the Town Marshal shall keep in mind the
physical layout of the street, whether or not additional parking has been
permitted currently in that area, and any other factor bearing upon the smooth
flow of traffic and the health and safety of the residents of the area in
considering whether or not to grant a visitor’s permit.
(1985 Code, § 443-15) (Ord. 85-6, passed 4-15-1985)
§ 72.04 EMERGENCY CONDITIONS; PARKING PROHIBITION.
   (A)   In the event weather conditions or the conditions of town streets make
it necessary for parking on town streets to be prohibited or restricted for
snow plowing, snow removal, street maintenance, or related purposes, the Town
Council or such person as it designates, as authorized by I.C. 9-21-1-3, shall
implement a parking prohibition on part or all of the streets in the town as
the Town Council or designated person finds necessary.
   (B)   The parking prohibition shall not be effective until 12 hours after
signs have been posted stating the hours in which the prohibition will be in
effect. The parking prohibition signs shall be posted at regular intervals
along the streets on which parking will be prohibited so that they are clearly
visible along the streets. The parking prohibition shall terminate at the time
stated on the signs or upon their removal by authorized persons.
   (C)   Officers of the Town Police Department are authorized to remove or
have removed from a street any vehicle that is parked in violation of this
section.
   (D)   Any person who violates the provisions of this section commits a class
E ordinance violation and shall be fined. Each day of violation continued shall
constitute a separate violation.
(1985 Code, § 443-25) (Ord. 79-6, passed 1-21-1980; Am. Ord. passed 6-15-1981)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 72.05 VIOLATIONS; ENFORCEMENT.
   (A)   The officers of the Town Police Department shall attach to vehicles
parked in violation of this chapter a notice to the owner or operator thereof
that the vehicle has been parked in violation of this chapter or, in the
alternative, shall personally deliver or tender the notice to the owner or
operator.
   (B)   (1)   A person who violates the provisions of this chapter commits a
class E ordinance violation, and a fine shall be entered against the violator.
      (2)   For the purpose of this section, the following definition shall
apply unless the context clearly indicates or requires a different meaning.
         BUSINESS DAY. Monday through Friday, excluding town holidays.
   (C)   In lieu of any person being charged in court for a violation of this
chapter, the person may appear in person or by attorney or agent in the office
of the Town Clerk-Treasurer within ten days after the issuance of a notice and
admit liability for the payment of the fine provided by this section and tender
the sum owed. The payment of a fine is an acknowledgment of a violation of this
chapter, and the Clerk-Treasurer shall issue a receipt to the violator
acknowledging payment therefor.
   (D)   If a person alleged to have violated this chapter fails to pay the sum
owed to the Clerk-Treasurer as provided in this section, the person shall be
charged in court for the violation. The fine shall be recovered in an action in
the name of the corporate town in any court having competent jurisdiction. This
chapter shall be enforced by the town through its Attorney.
(1985 Code, § 443-50) (Ord. 79-5, passed 1-21-1980; Ord. 79-6, passed 1-21-
1980; Am. Ord. passed 6-15-1981; Am. Ord. 93-6, passed 7-21-1993; Am. Ord. 06-
16, passed 9-11-2006)
Penalty, see § 10.99
§ 72.06 UNPAID FINES; IMPOUNDMENT.
   (A)   Whenever a car, truck, or vehicle is illegally parked on the street
and there are held by the proper authority of the town five or more unpaid
parking violations for that car, truck, or vehicle, the police of the town
shall cause that car, truck, or vehicle to be removed to a garage or place of
storage within the town, and the same shall not be released until the owner
thereof produces to the Police Department of the town proper evidence of
ownership and pays all unpaid parking violations against that car, truck, or
vehicle, together with the costs of the impounding of the vehicle.
   (B)   For the purpose of this section, the following definition shall apply
unless the context clearly indicates or requires a different meaning.
      ILLEGALLY PARKED. Any violation of the parking provisions of this code
pertaining to parking within the town.
(1985 Code, § 443-55) (Ord. passed 6-15-1981)
Penalty, see § 10.99
